t = Path("docs/TRACK.md"); s = t.read_text()
A = """

### 3.116 The swing charts; the founder's second marks; the sideways rule (25 Nov 22:10)
**Third version, first run** (commit 6a4189e; a first attempt crashed on SNDK, which has 447 sessions where the code assumed 620 -- Claude's test had no short history; fixed). 342 swings on 20 stocks, about 17 a stock. Of the 18 swings taken from the founder's first marks, 14 are reproduced within 9 days at both ends; the 4 not reproduced are near misses on dates (AAPL two, TSM one, AMD one).
**The founder's second marks (25 Nov 21:18), word for word:** 'NVDA has a mistake on 5, which should actually end in the middle of that area, because the later part of the 5th's area is sideway movements. 10th also has the same problem, should have ended at the trough before 2026 Jan, the remaining part is also sideway movements. AAPL is correct. TSM also correct.' (NVDA 5: up 6 Sep 2024 to 6 Jan 2025, 83 sessions, +45%, strength 1.28; NVDA 10: down 29 Oct 2025 to 30 Mar 2026, 103 sessions, -20%, strength 0.71.)
**What they say.** A swing runs to the most extreme price, and a long flat stretch can end on a price slightly more extreme than where the move really ended. His eye ends the trend where the move ended and calls the rest sideways.
**First sideways rule, withdrawn the same evening.** 'The move ended on the day up to which its strength from the start is highest; cut what follows when it is at least 5 sessions and under one chance drift.' Founder, 25 Nov 21:31, with the NVIDIA chart marked by hand: 'AAPL TSM AMD SNDK don't look right, I would prefer the previous version for these 4 stocks.' Two faults, both Claude's: (1) that rule ends a trend after its first sharp days, because a short sharp start has the highest strength; (2) the run re-chose K each time and the sideways cuts changed the score, so the charts were drawn with K = 6 where the version he had accepted used K = 5 (marks reproduced fell from 14 to 9 of 18).
**Second sideways rule, conservative (to be confirmed on the charts).** K is fixed at 5. For a swing: take the best price reached in its first part, leaving a last part of at least 10 sessions and at least a third of the swing. If that price is already within 1.5 usual daily moves of the swing's final extreme, the trend ends there and the last part is 'sideways'. Every other swing stays exactly as in the third version. This matches the founder's two hand-drawn lines on NVIDIA (the rise from Sep 2024 ends at the November high, not at the January one; the fall from May 2026 ends at the June low, not at the July one). **Three numbers are in the rule (10 sessions, a third, 1.5 usual moves); all three are Claude's and fitted to three examples on one stock.** The check is again the stocks the founder has not marked.
"""
